# Copy to docker/env/build.env on the build host. Values here are build-only.
# 0: use direct official downloads; 1: use an existing reverse SSH tunnel.
SSH_TUNNEL_ENABLED=0
SSH_TUNNEL_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:17897

# To use a proxy already running on your Windows workstation at 127.0.0.1:7897:
# 1. Run in a LOCAL PowerShell window and leave it open:
#    ssh -N -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes -o ServerAliveInterval=30 -R 127.0.0.1:17897:127.0.0.1:7897 cdky@192.168.88.91
# 2. On 91, set SSH_TUNNEL_ENABLED=1 above, then run docker.sh fetch/build.
# 3. After downloads/builds finish, Ctrl+C the local ssh command.
# Replace the user, SSH host and local proxy port for your machine (92 works too).
# The reverse listener binds only to server loopback. The build uses host networking
# to reach it; neither the proxy address nor SSH credentials are stored in images.
# A Dockerfile cannot open this workstation-to-server tunnel: start it on the workstation.
